body:
This root stack config now depends on `./examples/hotcrm-bridge.js`, which (a) imports HotCRM `.ts` source files directly and (b) relies on resolving `@hotcrm/ai` via a Vite-only alias. If `objectstack.config.ts` is loaded in a non-Vite Node context (CLI/runtime config evaluation), it can fail to load due to unresolved TS imports and/or the `@hotcrm/ai` module. Consider making the `@hotcrm/ai` stub resolvable in Node resolution too (e.g., add a real workspace package named `@hotcrm/ai` with `package.json`/exports), and/or avoid importing TS source from JS by pointing the bridge at built JS entrypoints or gating HotCRM imports behind a build-time-only path.
```suggestion

// HotCRM bridge is optional: it may not be loadable in non-Vite Node contexts.
let hotcrmObjects: any[] = [];
let hotcrmApps: any[] = [];
let mergeObjects: (baseObjects: any[], extraObjects: any[]) => any[] = (baseObjects, extraObjects) => [
  ...baseObjects,
  ...extraObjects,
];

try {
  // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-var-requires
  // Use dynamic require so that plain Node runtimes are not hard-coupled to the Vite-only bridge.
  const hotcrmBridge = require('./examples/hotcrm-bridge.js');
  if (hotcrmBridge) {
    hotcrmObjects = hotcrmBridge.hotcrmObjects || hotcrmObjects;
    hotcrmApps = hotcrmBridge.hotcrmApps || hotcrmApps;
    mergeObjects = hotcrmBridge.mergeObjects || mergeObjects;
  }
} catch {
  // HotCRM is not available in this environment; proceed without it.
}
```

`// @ts-nocheck` disables all TypeScript checking for this file, which can mask real integration mistakes (e.g., changes in HotCRM plugin exports). If the main goal is to prevent `tsc` from traversing HotCRM sources, prefer a more targeted approach: keep the `.d.ts` for the public surface, and replace file-wide `@ts-nocheck` with narrower suppressions (or restructure the bridge to avoid importing TS sources directly). This retains some signal for accidental breakages while still avoiding strict-mode errors from the submodule.
```suggestion
// @ts-expect-error HotCRM plugins are built separately; rely on their .d.ts surface only.
import { CRMPlugin } from './hotcrm/packages/crm/src/plugin.ts';
